1
00:00:00,337 --> 00:00:03,242
Monday morning. Your
alarm goes off at seven.

2
00:00:03,262 --> 00:00:04,342
You grab your bag.

3
00:00:04,362 --> 00:00:05,594
You catch the bus.

4
00:00:05,614 --> 00:00:08,669
And for the next six
hours, you sit at a desk.

5
00:00:08,689 --> 00:00:11,269
In a room full of kids your own age.

6
00:00:11,289 --> 00:00:13,570
Facing one adult, at the front.

7
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:15,015
There's a bell. A timetable.

8
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:17,219
And tests. Lots of tests.

9
00:00:17,239 --> 00:00:21,933
In rich countries, school adds up to
about seven and a half thousand hours of

10
00:00:21,953 --> 00:00:25,185
lessons, and that's
only by your mid teens.

11
00:00:25,205 --> 00:00:27,798
It feels like school has always existed.

12
00:00:27,818 --> 00:00:33,133
It hasn't. For almost all of human
history, there were no schools at all.

13
00:00:33,153 --> 00:00:38,169
Most children only started going to
school in the last two hundred years.

14
00:00:38,189 --> 00:00:40,719
Now take time back fifty thousand years.

15
00:00:40,739 --> 00:00:43,182
A five year old girl wakes up in a camp.

16
00:00:43,202 --> 00:00:44,844
No school. No teacher.

17
00:00:44,864 --> 00:00:49,998
No books. Writing won't be invented
for another forty five thousand years.

18
00:00:50,018 --> 00:00:54,315
And yet, by the time she's grown up,
she'll need to know an enormous amount.

19
00:00:54,335 --> 00:00:57,240
Which plants are food,
and which ones are poison.

20
00:00:57,260 --> 00:00:58,528
Where to find water.

21
00:00:58,548 --> 00:01:00,353
How to read an animal's tracks.

22
00:01:00,373 --> 00:01:02,614
How to keep a baby alive.

23
00:01:02,634 --> 00:01:05,664
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

24
00:01:05,684 --> 00:01:07,389
Their kids didn't learn much.

25
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:10,015
They just copied their
parents, and grunted.

26
00:01:10,035 --> 00:01:11,691
At least, that's what we assume.

27
00:01:11,711 --> 00:01:14,247
But ancient children
left clues everywhere.

28
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:18,085
Tiny finger marks, high
on the ceilings of caves.

29
00:01:18,105 --> 00:01:22,747
A trail of footprints where someone carried
a toddler for more than a kilometre.

30
00:01:22,767 --> 00:01:26,751
And kids today who gather their
own food before they can read.

31
00:01:26,771 --> 00:01:29,626
They all point to the
oldest classroom on Earth.

32
00:01:29,646 --> 00:01:31,821
And it didn't have any walls.

33
00:01:31,841 --> 00:01:34,197
First, here's something
strange about humans.

34
00:01:34,217 --> 00:01:37,026
We take longer to grow up
than any other primate.

35
00:01:37,046 --> 00:01:42,801
A wild chimpanzee usually has her first
baby at around thirteen to fifteen.

36
00:01:42,821 --> 00:01:48,637
In foraging societies, women typically
have their first child at around nineteen.

37
00:01:48,657 --> 00:01:51,948
Why would an animal stay small
and helpless for so long?

38
00:01:51,968 --> 00:01:53,683
Part of the answer is the brain.

39
00:01:53,703 --> 00:01:59,066
In twenty fourteen, scientists worked
out how much energy a child's brain uses.

40
00:01:59,086 --> 00:02:04,128
At around four years old, it uses energy
equal to about two thirds of what the

41
00:02:04,148 --> 00:02:06,416
whole body burns at rest.

42
00:02:06,436 --> 00:02:11,053
Two thirds. And that's the same
age when body growth is slowest.

43
00:02:11,073 --> 00:02:14,981
Scientists think the child is
putting its energy into its brain.

44
00:02:15,001 --> 00:02:16,768
So what is all that brain power for?

45
00:02:16,788 --> 00:02:21,416
Learning. And the hardest thing you ever
learned, you learned without a school.

46
00:02:21,436 --> 00:02:23,149
Your first language.

47
00:02:23,169 --> 00:02:27,736
By the time children start school,
they already know thousands of words.

48
00:02:27,756 --> 00:02:31,216
Around ten thousand, by
one well known estimate.

49
00:02:31,236 --> 00:02:33,855
Nobody sat them down at
a desk to learn them.

50
00:02:33,875 --> 00:02:37,299
They picked them up by listening,
and talking, and playing.

51
00:02:37,319 --> 00:02:40,679
And in the hunter-gatherer world,
there's a lot more to learn.

52
00:02:40,699 --> 00:02:46,129
The Ju'hoansi of the Kalahari recognise
more than a hundred wild plants as food.

53
00:02:46,149 --> 00:02:49,865
Roots, berries, nuts, melons and gums.

54
00:02:49,885 --> 00:02:51,138
Then there's hunting.

55
00:02:51,158 --> 00:02:55,783
In twenty twenty, researchers looked at
records from more than eighteen hundred

56
00:02:55,803 --> 00:02:59,201
hunters, in forty
societies around the world.

57
00:02:59,221 --> 00:03:02,776
Hunters didn't reach their best
until around their thirties.

58
00:03:02,796 --> 00:03:05,091
Years after they'd stopped growing.

59
00:03:05,111 --> 00:03:06,641
Hunting isn't just strength.

60
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:10,816
It's knowledge. Tracking, patience,
knowing how animals think.

61
00:03:10,836 --> 00:03:13,206
And that takes decades to learn.

62
00:03:13,226 --> 00:03:15,168
Other animals have to learn too.

63
00:03:15,188 --> 00:03:16,825
Look at a young chimpanzee.

64
00:03:16,845 --> 00:03:19,250
Chimps fish for termites with a twig.

65
00:03:19,270 --> 00:03:23,011
They poke it into the mound, and
pull it out covered in termites.

66
00:03:23,031 --> 00:03:27,250
At Gombe in Tanzania, scientists
watched young chimps learn this.

67
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:31,825
Young females spent more time watching
their mothers fish, while young males

68
00:03:31,845 --> 00:03:33,573
spent more time playing.

69
00:03:33,593 --> 00:03:37,816
The daughters learned the skill more
than two years earlier, on average.

70
00:03:37,836 --> 00:03:39,703
Cracking nuts is even harder.

71
00:03:39,723 --> 00:03:43,008
A chimp needs a stone hammer,
a flat rock, and good aim.

72
00:03:43,028 --> 00:03:47,584
In Guinea, young chimps only start cracking
nuts at around three and a half to

73
00:03:47,604 --> 00:03:52,197
five years old. Before that, they
steal the kernels from their mothers.

74
00:03:52,217 --> 00:03:55,545
And they keep getting better
until they're about eleven.

75
00:03:55,565 --> 00:03:56,562
But here's the thing.

76
00:03:56,582 --> 00:03:58,381
Chimp mothers rarely teach.

77
00:03:58,401 --> 00:04:01,942
The young ones mostly have to
watch, and figure it out alone.

78
00:04:01,962 --> 00:04:04,935
Real teaching is surprisingly
rare in animals.

79
00:04:04,955 --> 00:04:07,473
One famous example comes from meerkats.

80
00:04:07,493 --> 00:04:11,298
Adult meerkats give their pups
scorpions to practise on.

81
00:04:11,318 --> 00:04:13,085
At first, dead ones.

82
00:04:13,105 --> 00:04:15,698
Then live ones, with the sting bitten off.

83
00:04:15,718 --> 00:04:17,960
And finally, the real thing.

84
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:22,319
If the scorpion crawls away, the
adult nudges it back to the pup.

85
00:04:22,339 --> 00:04:24,543
Humans take teaching much further.

86
00:04:24,563 --> 00:04:27,595
And it starts with the
youngest learners of all.

87
00:04:27,615 --> 00:04:30,074
A human baby is born almost helpless.

88
00:04:30,094 --> 00:04:34,050
And for most of history, it wasn't
raised by its mother alone.

89
00:04:34,070 --> 00:04:38,242
The anthropologist Sarah Hrdy
calls humans cooperative breeders.

90
00:04:38,262 --> 00:04:39,380
Grandmothers. Aunts.

91
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:42,077
Fathers. Older brothers and sisters.

92
00:04:42,097 --> 00:04:47,383
In many societies, older children do much
of the carrying and minding of babies.

93
00:04:47,403 --> 00:04:52,742
Among the Efe people of Central Africa,
researchers watched who held the babies.

94
00:04:52,762 --> 00:04:57,354
An Efe baby had about fourteen
different caretakers, on average.

95
00:04:57,374 --> 00:05:02,079
By three weeks old, babies spent around
forty percent of their time with someone

96
00:05:02,099 --> 00:05:03,585
other than their mother.

97
00:05:03,605 --> 00:05:06,066
Every one of those people was a teacher.

98
00:05:06,086 --> 00:05:08,493
And sometimes, we can
see them in the ground.

99
00:05:08,513 --> 00:05:13,506
At White Sands, in New Mexico, ancient
footprints are preserved in what was once

100
00:05:13,526 --> 00:05:18,388
mud. More than ten thousand years ago,
someone walked across it for more than a

101
00:05:18,408 --> 00:05:19,944
kilometre and a half.

102
00:05:19,964 --> 00:05:22,800
Probably a young woman, or a teenager.

103
00:05:22,820 --> 00:05:28,027
Every so often, a set of tiny toddler
footprints appears beside theirs.

104
00:05:28,047 --> 00:05:30,777
They were carrying a child of about two.

105
00:05:30,797 --> 00:05:35,164
Now and then, they set the child down,
maybe to rest their arms, then picked it

106
00:05:35,184 --> 00:05:37,304
up again, and kept going.

107
00:05:37,324 --> 00:05:41,145
Later, a giant ground sloth and
a mammoth crossed the same trail.

108
00:05:41,165 --> 00:05:44,470
Then, hours later, the walker
came back the other way.

109
00:05:44,490 --> 00:05:46,181
Without the child.

110
00:05:46,201 --> 00:05:47,831
We'll never know their story.

111
00:05:47,851 --> 00:05:51,192
But it's a moment of
childhood, frozen in mud.

112
00:05:51,212 --> 00:05:53,332
So how did children actually learn?

113
00:05:53,352 --> 00:05:58,545
In two thousand nine, the psychologist
Peter Gray asked anthropologists who had

114
00:05:58,565 --> 00:06:00,495
lived with hunter-gatherers.

115
00:06:00,515 --> 00:06:03,010
How did the children spend their days?

116
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:05,380
The answer, again and again, was play.

117
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:09,180
Children were free to play
almost all day, every day.

118
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:14,167
One researcher said Efe boys played nearly
all the time, until they were fifteen

119
00:06:14,187 --> 00:06:18,361
or more. And they didn't play in
a class of kids their own age.

120
00:06:18,381 --> 00:06:20,186
They played in mixed groups.

121
00:06:20,206 --> 00:06:23,158
Toddlers, kids and teenagers together.

122
00:06:23,178 --> 00:06:27,492
In a small band, there might only be
one or two other children your age.

123
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:30,167
The big kids looked after the little ones.

124
00:06:30,187 --> 00:06:32,841
And the little ones copied the big ones.

125
00:06:32,861 --> 00:06:35,440
And their games looked
a lot like adult work.

126
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:40,315
Among the Hadza of Tanzania, boys get
their first small bows when they're about

127
00:06:40,335 --> 00:06:45,979
three. They spend years shooting at
targets, at birds, and at small animals.

128
00:06:45,999 --> 00:06:50,841
Hadza children, from around five years
old, go out in groups to gather food.

129
00:06:50,861 --> 00:06:52,604
Baobab fruit. Berries.

130
00:06:52,624 --> 00:06:57,645
Small roots. They eat some of it on the
spot, and bring the rest back to camp.

131
00:06:57,665 --> 00:06:59,695
Girls tend to bring more home.

132
00:06:59,715 --> 00:07:02,461
Boys tend to eat more on the way.

133
00:07:02,481 --> 00:07:04,150
And it's not just the Hadza.

134
00:07:04,170 --> 00:07:09,385
In the forests of Central Africa,
researchers filmed Aka babies, just twelve to

135
00:07:09,405 --> 00:07:10,946
fourteen months old.

136
00:07:10,966 --> 00:07:14,351
They counted about eleven
moments of teaching every hour.

137
00:07:14,371 --> 00:07:19,526
Babies were handed real digging sticks,
knives, and even small axes, and shown

138
00:07:19,546 --> 00:07:22,685
how to use them. To us,
that sounds dangerous.

139
00:07:22,705 --> 00:07:24,898
To them, it's just growing up.

140
00:07:24,918 --> 00:07:26,894
Children may even have had toys.

141
00:07:26,914 --> 00:07:32,111
In the Arctic, about eight hundred years
ago, Inuit ancestors left behind tiny

142
00:07:32,131 --> 00:07:35,786
harpoons, kayaks, sledges and dolls.

143
00:07:35,806 --> 00:07:38,556
Children's versions of the adult world.

144
00:07:38,576 --> 00:07:43,256
And from France, more than fifteen thousand
years ago, there's a little bone disc

145
00:07:43,276 --> 00:07:45,456
with a deer on each side.

146
00:07:45,476 --> 00:07:50,081
Some archaeologists think that if you spun
it on a string, the animal would seem

147
00:07:50,101 --> 00:07:53,034
to move. It might have been a toy.

148
00:07:53,054 --> 00:07:55,161
Children also left their footprints.

149
00:07:55,181 --> 00:08:00,186
On a beach in Normandy, France, scientists
found two hundred and fifty seven

150
00:08:00,206 --> 00:08:01,924
Neanderthal footprints.

151
00:08:01,944 --> 00:08:04,386
About eighty thousand years old.

152
00:08:04,406 --> 00:08:07,380
Most of them were made by
children and teenagers.

153
00:08:07,400 --> 00:08:09,253
The youngest was about two.

154
00:08:09,273 --> 00:08:12,706
Neanderthal kids, on the
shore, just like kids today.

155
00:08:12,726 --> 00:08:15,820
Children even left their
marks deep inside caves.

156
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:20,559
In Rouffignac Cave, in France, the
walls and ceilings are covered in finger

157
00:08:20,579 --> 00:08:25,714
lines. Drawn in soft clay, more
than thirteen thousand years ago.

158
00:08:25,734 --> 00:08:30,527
Researchers measured the width of the
finger marks, and compared them with lines

159
00:08:30,547 --> 00:08:34,186
drawn in clay by more than
a hundred modern children.

160
00:08:34,206 --> 00:08:37,487
Many were made by children
as young as two to five.

161
00:08:37,507 --> 00:08:42,337
And some of the lines are high up on the
ceiling, far too high for a small child

162
00:08:42,357 --> 00:08:45,558
to reach. They were
probably held up by adults.

163
00:08:45,578 --> 00:08:49,741
Picture it. An adult, holding a child up
in the flickering lamplight, while the

164
00:08:49,761 --> 00:08:52,509
child traces lines in the clay.

165
00:08:52,529 --> 00:08:53,534
Maybe it was a game.

166
00:08:53,554 --> 00:08:55,120
Maybe it meant something.

167
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:57,252
Either way, the children were there.

168
00:08:57,272 --> 00:09:03,068
In Chauvet Cave, a young person, perhaps
a child, walked along a passage, wiping

169
00:09:03,088 --> 00:09:05,804
a burning torch on the wall along the way.

170
00:09:05,824 --> 00:09:10,296
The charcoal from that torch is
about twenty six thousand years old.

171
00:09:10,316 --> 00:09:13,571
And in twenty thirteen,
something remarkable happened.

172
00:09:13,591 --> 00:09:19,258
Three expert trackers from the Ju'hoansi
San of Namibia were invited into Ice Age

173
00:09:19,278 --> 00:09:24,313
caves in France. They read the ancient
footprints the way they'd read tracks in

174
00:09:24,333 --> 00:09:26,538
the Kalahari. Who walked here.

175
00:09:26,558 --> 00:09:27,826
How old they were.

176
00:09:27,846 --> 00:09:29,157
Where they were going.

177
00:09:29,177 --> 00:09:31,844
They identified at least
twenty four people.

178
00:09:31,864 --> 00:09:34,702
Ten of them were children or teenagers.

179
00:09:34,722 --> 00:09:39,736
In one cave, they read the prints of a
man and a teenage boy, carrying clay to

180
00:09:39,756 --> 00:09:41,783
sculpt a pair of bison.

181
00:09:41,803 --> 00:09:44,858
Not every scientist agrees
with their readings.

182
00:09:44,878 --> 00:09:48,121
But skills like theirs
are learned the same way.

183
00:09:48,141 --> 00:09:51,832
By watching, copying,
and following the adults.

184
00:09:51,852 --> 00:09:54,107
Some skills are much harder to pick up.

185
00:09:54,127 --> 00:09:55,999
Like making stone tools.

186
00:09:56,019 --> 00:09:59,374
Hit the stone wrong, and you
get nothing but useless chips.

187
00:09:59,394 --> 00:10:03,943
Or a cut finger. Modern researchers
have tried to learn it themselves.

188
00:10:03,963 --> 00:10:09,163
In one study, twenty six beginners
practised making stone hand axes for up to

189
00:10:09,183 --> 00:10:14,722
ninety hours. They improved fast for the
first thirty hours, and then got stuck.

190
00:10:14,742 --> 00:10:17,606
Most still weren't as good as the experts.

191
00:10:17,626 --> 00:10:19,383
So how did ancient kids learn?

192
00:10:19,403 --> 00:10:24,396
At Etiolles, near Paris, people camped
by a river about fifteen thousand years

193
00:10:24,416 --> 00:10:29,693
ago. Archaeologists fitted the broken
flint back together, flake by flake.

194
00:10:29,713 --> 00:10:32,170
Like a three-dimensional jigsaw.

195
00:10:32,190 --> 00:10:34,170
And they found two kinds of work.

196
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:38,020
Master knappers, working the best
flint by the central hearth.

197
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:42,920
And beginners, practising on second-rate
blocks at the edges of the camp.

198
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:46,507
Some of the beginners' work
is clumsy and heavy-handed.

199
00:10:46,527 --> 00:10:49,505
Some is nearly as good as the masters '.

200
00:10:49,525 --> 00:10:53,384
It looks like children and teenagers
were learning there, within sight of the

201
00:10:53,404 --> 00:10:56,221
experts. And how you teach matters.

202
00:10:56,241 --> 00:11:00,820
In twenty fifteen, researchers taught
nearly two hundred volunteers to make

203
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:02,517
simple stone flakes.

204
00:11:02,537 --> 00:11:04,192
Some worked it out on their own.

205
00:11:04,212 --> 00:11:05,792
Some watched an expert.

206
00:11:05,812 --> 00:11:07,865
And some were taught with words.

207
00:11:07,885 --> 00:11:09,727
Just watching barely helped.

208
00:11:09,747 --> 00:11:12,702
Teaching with words nearly
doubled their results.

209
00:11:12,722 --> 00:11:17,788
Some scientists think teaching may even
be one of the reasons language evolved.

210
00:11:17,808 --> 00:11:20,385
And the best time for
words was after dark.

211
00:11:20,405 --> 00:11:25,147
In the Kalahari, the anthropologist Polly
Wiessner recorded what the Ju'hoansi

212
00:11:25,167 --> 00:11:28,394
talked about. During
the day, and at night.

213
00:11:28,414 --> 00:11:32,329
In the daytime, much of the talk
was about work, and complaints.

214
00:11:32,349 --> 00:11:34,578
Who didn't share. Who was lazy.

215
00:11:34,598 --> 00:11:38,489
Fewer than one conversation
in ten involved a story.

216
00:11:38,509 --> 00:11:42,994
But by the fire at night, more than three
quarters of the long conversations were

217
00:11:43,014 --> 00:11:46,409
stories. Stories about people far away.

218
00:11:46,429 --> 00:11:49,464
About hunts, and mistakes,
and how to behave.

219
00:11:49,484 --> 00:11:52,118
That's where children learned
the rules of their world.

220
00:11:52,138 --> 00:11:54,002
And good storytellers mattered.

221
00:11:54,022 --> 00:11:58,690
Among the Agta of the Philippines,
skilled storytellers were about twice as

222
00:11:58,710 --> 00:12:01,277
likely to be chosen as campmates.

223
00:12:01,297 --> 00:12:04,665
And on average, they had
more surviving children.

224
00:12:04,685 --> 00:12:07,767
Stories can also last an
astonishingly long time.

225
00:12:07,787 --> 00:12:13,325
In Australia, Aboriginal stories from
twenty one places along the coast seem to

226
00:12:13,345 --> 00:12:16,212
describe the sea drowning the land.

227
00:12:16,232 --> 00:12:20,077
Something that happened more
than seven thousand years ago.

228
00:12:20,097 --> 00:12:24,890
That's hundreds of generations, passed
down from one child to the next, with no

229
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:28,350
writing at all. And those
memory skills still work.

230
00:12:28,370 --> 00:12:33,350
In twenty twenty one, medical students
in Australia were taught an Aboriginal

231
00:12:33,370 --> 00:12:36,025
memory method for about half an hour.

232
00:12:36,045 --> 00:12:41,095
They linked a list of twenty items to
places in a landscape, and to a story.

233
00:12:41,115 --> 00:12:45,620
Afterwards, they were roughly three times
more likely to remember the whole list

234
00:12:45,640 --> 00:12:50,128
perfectly. And in the Pacific,
knowledge like this could cross oceans.

235
00:12:50,148 --> 00:12:55,311
In nineteen seventy six, the navigator
Mau Piailug guided a voyaging canoe from

236
00:12:55,331 --> 00:13:00,748
Hawaii to Tahiti. About four thousand
kilometres, with no compass and no

237
00:13:00,768 --> 00:13:03,890
map. He read the stars,
the swells, and the birds.

238
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:08,307
His grandfather had started teaching
him when he was a small child.

239
00:13:08,327 --> 00:13:10,107
So when did schools appear?

240
00:13:10,127 --> 00:13:14,174
When something came along that you
couldn't learn just by watching.

241
00:13:14,194 --> 00:13:19,212
More than five thousand years ago, in
Mesopotamia, people invented writing.

242
00:13:19,232 --> 00:13:21,854
Pressed into clay tablets with a reed.

243
00:13:21,874 --> 00:13:23,029
Hundreds of signs.

244
00:13:23,049 --> 00:13:28,236
Years to master. By about four thousand
years ago, Sumerian scribes were trained

245
00:13:28,256 --> 00:13:31,588
in schools. They called
them tablet houses.

246
00:13:31,608 --> 00:13:35,328
Pupils sat on benches and
copied texts, over and over.

247
00:13:35,348 --> 00:13:37,191
And one of their stories survives.

248
00:13:37,211 --> 00:13:39,535
It's about a schoolboy's bad day.

249
00:13:39,555 --> 00:13:41,485
He's late. He makes mistakes.

250
00:13:41,505 --> 00:13:44,202
And, again and again,
he gets beaten for it.

251
00:13:44,222 --> 00:13:48,577
Then his father invites the teacher home
for dinner, and gives him presents.

252
00:13:48,597 --> 00:13:52,845
And suddenly, the teacher has
nothing but praise for the boy.

253
00:13:52,865 --> 00:13:55,682
School, it seems, was
tough from the very start.

254
00:13:55,702 --> 00:13:58,682
For thousands of years,
school was only for a few.

255
00:13:58,702 --> 00:14:01,760
Scribes. Priests. The
children of the rich.

256
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:06,835
Then, in seventeen sixty three, Prussia
ordered children in the countryside to go

257
00:14:06,855 --> 00:14:10,883
to school. Massachusetts
followed in eighteen fifty two.

258
00:14:10,903 --> 00:14:12,746
England in eighteen eighty.

259
00:14:12,766 --> 00:14:14,933
Rows of desks. A bell.

260
00:14:14,953 --> 00:14:18,352
One teacher, facing a
class of the same age.

261
00:14:18,372 --> 00:14:19,694
That's the school you know.

262
00:14:19,714 --> 00:14:22,155
And in human terms, it's brand new.

263
00:14:22,175 --> 00:14:26,825
Even today, about two hundred and seventy
three million children and young people

264
00:14:26,845 --> 00:14:28,410
are out of school.

265
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:30,783
So what did ancient children have instead?

266
00:14:30,803 --> 00:14:35,773
Researchers who study hunter-gatherer
children today keep finding the same thing.

267
00:14:35,793 --> 00:14:37,673
Children learn mostly by watching.

268
00:14:37,693 --> 00:14:39,659
By copying. And by playing.

269
00:14:39,679 --> 00:14:43,659
Adults do teach, especially
hard skills like hunting.

270
00:14:43,679 --> 00:14:48,372
But in Hadza and BaYaka camps, children
taught each other more often than adults

271
00:14:48,392 --> 00:14:51,080
taught them. Think about what that means.

272
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:52,448
There was no classroom.

273
00:14:52,468 --> 00:14:55,037
Because the whole camp was the classroom.

274
00:14:55,057 --> 00:14:56,725
Every adult was a teacher.

275
00:14:56,745 --> 00:14:58,830
Every older kid was a teacher.

276
00:14:58,850 --> 00:15:01,591
Every walk to the water hole was a lesson.

277
00:15:01,611 --> 00:15:04,291
Every story by the fire was a lesson.

278
00:15:04,311 --> 00:15:07,207
And the lessons didn't stop
at the end of the day.

279
00:15:07,227 --> 00:15:08,093
There was no bell.

280
00:15:08,113 --> 00:15:10,505
No tests. But there was a final exam.

281
00:15:10,525 --> 00:15:15,917
Survival. And for three hundred thousand
years, children passed it, generation

282
00:15:15,937 --> 00:15:17,707
after generation.

283
00:15:17,727 --> 00:15:21,557
Today, children learn things no
hunter-gatherer ever needed.

284
00:15:21,577 --> 00:15:23,995
Reading. Algebra. Chemistry.

285
00:15:24,015 --> 00:15:26,884
And schools have done
amazing things for the world.

286
00:15:26,904 --> 00:15:30,080
But some of the oldest ways
of learning still work.

287
00:15:30,100 --> 00:15:31,630
Playing. Watching.

288
00:15:31,650 --> 00:15:33,092
Asking the older kids.

289
00:15:33,112 --> 00:15:34,950
Listening to stories.

290
00:15:34,970 --> 00:15:39,382
Fifty thousand years ago, a five
year old girl woke up in a camp.

291
00:15:39,402 --> 00:15:42,532
She had no books. No teacher
at the front of a room.

292
00:15:42,552 --> 00:15:46,069
But she had grandmothers,
and aunts, and older cousins.

293
00:15:46,089 --> 00:15:48,057
She had stories by the fire.

294
00:15:48,077 --> 00:15:50,589
And a whole world to practise on.

295
00:15:50,609 --> 00:15:51,414
You went to school.

296
00:15:51,434 --> 00:15:53,509
She grew up inside one.
